<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Florida</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Florida is a peninsula and state in the south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, between the Atlantic and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>. Named by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-ponce-de-leon">Juan Ponce de León</a> in 1513, it was a Spanish colony from the founding of St. Augustine in 1565, British from 1763 to 1783 and Spanish again until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> ceded it to the United States by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adams-onis-treaty">Adams–Onís Treaty</a> of 1819. It became a state in 1845 after the Seminole Wars.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/florida.jpg" alt="Florida" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Template:Brendon Tiana · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Florida.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Abraham Lincoln's election in November 1860 brought declarations of secession from seven slave states of the Deep South whose cotton economies depended on enslaved labour: South Carolina on 20 December 1860, unanimously, then Mississippi, <b>Florida</b>, Alabama, Georgia, Louisiana and Texas by 1 February 1861, none of them unanimous.</blockquote><a href="/empire/confederate-states-of-america">Confederate States of America · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Cuban revolution of 1959, emigrants carried Santería to Miami, New York and beyond, and in 1993 the Supreme Court of the United States upheld the right of a Santería church in <b>Florida</b> to practise animal sacrifice.</blockquote><a href="/religion/yoruba-religion">Yoruba religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>…II in Chile (about 14,500 years ago), Paisley Caves in Oregon, Cooper’s Ferry in Idaho (about 15,800 years ago), Page-Ladson in <b>Florida</b> and the Buttermilk Creek Complex in Texas are all considered older, and the White Sands footprints in New Mexico may date to around 23,000 years ago.</blockquote><a href="/culture/clovis-culture">Clovis culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>At its greatest extent the Hopewell exchange system ran from the northern shores of Lake Ontario to the Crystal River mounds in <b>Florida</b>, with most activity along the rivers that served as roads.</blockquote><a href="/culture/hopewell-culture">Hopewell culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Over the next two centuries the new way of life spread up the Mississippi to Wisconsin, where Aztalan, a palisaded town with platform mounds on the Crawfish River, was occupied until the 13th century, and through the South-East from the Arkansas River to Georgia and <b>Florida</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mississippian-culture">Mississippian culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Since the late 20th century descendants have reclaimed the name Taíno, or Boricua in Puerto Rico, describing traditions passed down in secret; by 2006 a couple of dozen organisations from <b>Florida</b> to Puerto Rico and California to New York formed what is called the Taíno restoration.</blockquote><a href="/culture/taino">Taíno · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
